Over time, rope became essential to nearly every society. Ancient Egyptian tomb paintings and surviving artifacts show ropes of papyrus and flax used for construction, rigging boats, and hauling stone for the pyramids. Mesopotamian, Chinese, and Andean cultures developed their own materials and methods, including hemp in China and cotton and agave fibers in the Americas. Andean peoples in particular produced remarkably strong suspension bridges from grass and plant cordage, some of which lasted for centuries and were maintained by communal labor.

